990234375, 0.02471923828125, -0.0173187255859375, -0.01322174072265625, -0.03173828125, -0.0340576171875, -0.012420654296875, -0.030975341796875, 0.041595458984375, 0.0254669189453125, 0.0191192626953125, 0.013702392578125, 0.0263671875, 0.0015249252319335938, -0.0006270408630371094, -0.049041748046875, 0.01107025146484375, 0.0010538101196289062, 0.006488800048828125, 0.03564453125, -0.0223236083984375, 0.0003409385681152344, -0.0157012939453125, -0.008941650390625, -0.005168914794921875, 0.0087432861328125, 0.0038604736328125, 0.01824951171875, -9.250640869140625e-05, 0.00977325439453125, -0.01334381103515625, 0.029052734375, -0.01290130615234375, 0.0184173583984375, -0.008270263671875, 0.00492095947265625, 0.005634307861328125, -0.03021240234375, -0.01551055908203125, -0.0116119384765625, -0.0201416015625, -0.02130126953125, -0.01424407958984375, 0.020355224609375, -0.043304443359375, 0.0106658935546875, 0.0017023086547851562, 0.007625579833984375, -0.00487518310546875, -0.005466461181640625, -0.0194854736328125, 0.01186370849609375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. While standard level-k models assume that level-0 players choose actions randomly or according to simple heuristics, recent evidence suggests that actual level-0 behavior may be more systematic. However, there is limited understanding of how level-0 actions and beliefs are formed and whether they align, especially in games where observed behavior deviates from standard predictions. Addressing this gap is important for refining models of strategic reasoning and improving their predictive accuracy.\n\nTo investigate these issues, we use the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), which involves groups of participants discussing and agreeing on actions and beliefs before making decisions in strategic games. This method allows us to directly elicit both the actions that participants consider characteristic of level-0 reasoning and their beliefs about what level-0 players would do, providing a richer picture of level-0 cognition than inferring it solely from observed choices.\n\nWe apply this approach to two settings. First, in the canonical beauty contest game, where players choose numbers between 0 and 100 and the winner is the one closest to two-thirds of the average, previous work (Burchardi and Penczynski, 2014) found that both elicited level-0 beliefs and observed level-0 actions cluster around 50, consistent with the standard assumption that level-0 players choose the midpoint. We replicate this result, finding that participants' stated beliefs about level-0 behavior and their actual level-0 choices both center on 50, indicating alignment between beliefs and actions in this context.\n\nSecond, we examine a variant of the 11-20 game introduced by Goeree et al. (2018), where players choose integers from 11 to 20 and payoffs are structured so that standard level-k reasoning predicts a particular pattern of choices. Goeree et al. (2018) observed that actual play in this game does not match the standard level-k predictions, suggesting that the standard level-0 assumption may not hold. In our study, we find that elicited level-0 beliefs (what participants think a level-0 player would do) and observed level-0 actions (what participants actually do when instructed to play as level-0) diverge: beliefs are dispersed across the available choices, while actions tend to concentrate on the lowest available number, 11. Both patterns differ from the standard assumption that level-0 players randomize uniformly. This divergence suggests that participants may use context-dependent heuristics or focal points when reasoning about level-0 behavior, providing a complementary explanation to the standard model by highlighting the role of salience and context in shaping level-0 choices.\n\nOur findings contribute to the literature by demonstrating that the alignment between level-0 beliefs and actions depends on the game context, and that standard assumptions about level-0 behavior may not generalize across games. This suggests that models of strategic reasoning should allow for more flexible, context-sensitive specifications of level-0 behavior. Future research could explore how different game features influence level-0 reasoning and test alternative models that incorporate these insights. Limitations of our study include the reliance on laboratory settings and specific game structures, which may affect generalizability. Nonetheless, our results underscore the importance of directly eliciting both actions and beliefs to better understand the foundations of strategic reasoning."}}